    "knowledge_point": "Solving for Two Coefficients",
    "problem": "The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+nx+d={x}^{2}-12x-8\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $n$ and $d$ are constants. What is the value of $n+d?$",
    "worked_solution": "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $n,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-12.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nn=-12.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $d,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-8.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nd=-8.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+d & =-12+(-8) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$",
